    "note": "Carried her off with Zeus' connivance to reign as queen of the dead; the abduction site and her yearly term below are disputed (persephone-abduction-details, persephone-split-year)"
  },
  {
    "id": "aphrodite-consort-hephaestus",
    "type": "consort",
    "from": "aphrodite",
    "to": "hephaestus",
    "sources": ["homer", "ovid"],
    "note": "His wife in the song of Demodocus (Odyssey 8) and in Ovid's tale of the net; in the Iliad his wife is Charis, in Hesiod Aglaia, youngest of the Graces"
  },
  {
    "id": "ares-lover-aphrodite",
    "type": "lover",
    "from": "ares",
    "to": "aphrodite",
    "sources": ["hesiod", "homer", "ovid"],
    "note": "The affair Helios exposed and Hephaestus snared in an unbreakable net; in Hesiod she bore Ares Phobos, Deimos and Harmonia (Theogony 933–937)"
  },
  {
    "id": "zeus-lover-demeter",
    "type": "lover",
    "from": "zeus",
    "to": "demeter",
    "sources": ["hesiod", "homer", "apollodorus"],
    "note": "The union that begot Persephone (Theogony 912)"
  },
  {
    "id": "zeus-lover-leto",
    "type": "lover",
    "from": "zeus",
    "to": "leto",
    "sources": ["hesiod", "homer", "apollodorus", "hyginus"],
    "note": "Joined in love with the lord of the aegis, Leto bore the archer twins Apollo and Artemis; Homer: Il. 14.327 (named in Zeus' catalogue of loves), Il. 1.9 (Apollo 'son of Leto and Zeus')"
  },
  {
    "id": "poseidon-lover-demeter",
    "type": "lover",
    "from": "poseidon",
    "to": "demeter",
    "sources": ["pausanias"],
    "note": "Arcadian tale from Thelpusa: he covered her as a stallion while she grazed as a mare among the herds of Oncius, an unwilling union; she bore the horse Arion and Despoina"
  },
  {
    "id": "zeus-adversary-cronus",
    "type": "adversary",
    "from": "zeus",
    "to": "cronus",
    "sources": ["hesiod", "homer", "apollodorus"],
    "note": "Forced his father to disgorge the swallowed children and, after the ten-year Titanomachy, thrust Cronus down beneath the earth and the unresting sea"
  },
  {
    "id": "cronus-adversary-uranus",
    "type": "adversary",
    "from": "cronus",
    "to": "uranus",
    "sources": ["hesiod", "apollodorus"],
    "note": "Alone dared answer Gaia's call: from ambush he reaped his father's manhood with the adamantine sickle"
  },
  {
    "id": "prometheus-adversary-zeus",
    "type": "adversary",
    "from": "prometheus",
    "to": "zeus",
    "sources": ["hesiod", "apollodorus", "hyginus"],
    "note": "Tricked Zeus over the portions at Mecone, stole fire in a fennel stalk, and was bound in the Caucasus with an eagle at his ever-regrowing liver"
  },
  {
    "id": "oceanus-ally-zeus",
    "type": "ally",
    "from": "oceanus",
    "to": "zeus",
    "sources": ["hesiod"],
    "note": "Sat out the Titanomachy; through his counsels his daughter Styx came first of all to Zeus' side and was honored for it (Theogony 389–403)"
  },
  {
    "id": "gaia-ally-zeus",
    "type": "ally",
    "from": "gaia",
    "to": "zeus",
    "sources": ["hesiod", "apollodorus"],
    "note": "Took the newborn to nurse in a Cretan cave, counselled the freeing of the Cyclopes, and prompted the gods to urge Zeus to kingship (Theogony 881–885)"
  },
  {
    "id": "gaia-adversary-zeus",
    "type": "adversary",
    "from": "gaia",
    "to": "zeus",
    "sources": ["hesiod", "apollodorus", "hyginus"],
    "note": "When the Titans had been driven from heaven she bore Typhoeus by Tartarus to challenge the new order; the motive of anger is explicit in Apollodorus"
  },
  {
    "id": "hera-adversary-leto",
    "type": "adversary",
    "from": "hera",
    "to": "leto",
    "sources": ["apollodorus", "hyginus"],
    "note": "Hunted the pregnant Titaness over the whole earth before the twins' birth on Delos; in Hyginus she decreed Latona give birth only where the sun never shone"
  },
  {
    "id": "athena-adversary-poseidon",
    "type": "adversary",
    "from": "athena",
    "to": "poseidon",
    "sources": ["apollodorus", "ovid", "pausanias"],
    "note": "The contest for Attica in the days of Cecrops: his well of sea-water against her first olive tree, judged for Athena"
  },
